A||||3||wallmic||Lost volumn in fuel tank?
Z||000000||wallmic||08-23-99||09:02 PM||||I posted this on the discussion forum and got some good information from ezsurf on repair of the fuel gage.  However, I believe the gage may be correct.  I run out of gas with the fuel gage at about 1/8 full. When I refill, I can only get about 8.5 gallons in! The tank should take 10.3 gal.  No apparent damage to the tank. My guess is the tank suction line is lifted off the tank bottom. Anyone seen this problem? Any easy fix without removing the tank? <br>    
Z||000001||GREENDANO||08-24-99||10:28 AM||||I have the same problem, I just make sure I reset my trip odometer after I fill the tank.  I plan on removing the tank this winter to repair the same problem.  I run out of gas, fill the tank and only get 8 gallons in.  I wish I could get the other 3, because I would really love to go a whole week without buying gas.<br>  Dan-O
Z||000002||David||08-31-99||08:40 AM||||If I run my '86 4 cyl completely out of gas, which happens at the 1/4 full mark on the gauge, I can only put in 9 gallons of fuel.  I now refuel at 200 miles and usually put in about 8 gallons.
Z||000003||Greg Piet||08-31-99||01:19 PM||||My fuel pump went out a week after getting my fiero.<br>If i remember correctly there is not an internal adjustment for the depth of the fuel pump.  I remember putting the strainer on the end which was about one inch high (rough guess)  I figured that was why it was advisable not to go beyond 8.5-gallons usage.<br>Besides I always thought it was a good idea not to go too low on fuel due to crud in the tank, example 96 octane C10 racing fuel from VP racing has a specific gravity of .760 @60 deg F.  Thus water will sink below the fuel (specific gravity about 1.0).  If the fuel line was on the very bottom of the tank, any water that got in the tank would then be sucked into the engine rather than petrol. Granted during driving the fuel tank gets jostled a bit which mixes some water in with the fuel, but imagine a cold start while the car has been sitting all night and the water has had time to settle to the bottom of the tank.  Unless you are certain that you wont get water in your tank(either from a shady gas station, or from another source), or you flush your gas tank regularly (I dont know many people that do that) I wouldnt reccomend modifying your fuel pump to implement another 30 or so miles per tank (approx).<br> 
